    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Selling a house as-is in Texas means you sell it in its present condition, without agreeing to make repairs or give warranties beyond those in the contract. It does not mean the buyer cannot inspect or walk away, and it does not excuse you from the seller's disclosure notice. Here is how as-is works under Texas law and the standard contract, and how to decide whether it is the right move for you.</p>

<h2>What "as-is" means legally in Texas</h2>
<p>Most Texas resales use the One to Four Family Residential Contract (Resale) promulgated by the Texas Real Estate Commission. In that contract, "As Is" means the present condition of the property with any and all defects and without warranty, except for the warranties of title and the warranties in the contract itself.</p>
<p>In plain English, the buyer agrees to take the house the way it is, and you are not promising to fix anything unless you specifically agree to. You are also not promising the house is free of problems you do not know about.</p>
<p>Texas courts generally enforce as-is clauses. In <em>Prudential Insurance Co. of America v. Jefferson Associates</em> (1995), the Texas Supreme Court held that a buyer who agrees to buy as-is generally cannot later claim they relied on the seller's statements about condition. The court also recognized important exceptions. An as-is agreement does not bind a buyer who was induced to sign by the seller's fraudulent representation or concealment, or whose inspection the seller impaired. That is why honest disclosure and the as-is clause work together.</p>

<h2>How the TREC contract handles an as-is sale</h2>
<p>The as-is language lives in Paragraph 7D of the TREC resale contract, titled "Acceptance of Property Condition." The buyer chooses one of two boxes:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>7D(1):</strong> Buyer accepts the property as-is.</li>
<li><strong>7D(2):</strong> Buyer accepts the property as-is, provided the seller, at the seller's expense, completes specific listed repairs and treatments.</li>
</ol>
<p>Under the second option, the repairs should be specific, such as "replace the water heater" or "repair the roof leak over the garage," not a catch-all like "fix anything the inspector finds." Vague repair language leads to disputes at closing.</p>
<p>Two other parts of the contract matter in every as-is deal:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inspections (Paragraph 7A):</strong> The seller must give the buyer and their inspectors reasonable access and keep the utilities on. Inspections are at the buyer's expense. This right is not tied to the option period.</li>
<li><strong>Seller's disclosure (Paragraph 7B):</strong> The contract records whether the buyer has received the seller's disclosure notice. If the buyer receives it after signing, the buyer may terminate within seven days.</li>
</ul>
<p>Form numbers and paragraph references change when TREC updates its contracts, so confirm the current version with your agent or attorney.</p>

<h2>The option period: why as-is buyers can still walk</h2>
<p>The biggest surprise for sellers is that an as-is contract does not lock the buyer in. Paragraph 5 of the TREC contract lets the buyer pay an option fee for an unrestricted right to terminate within a negotiated number of days after the effective date. During that window, the buyer can terminate for any reason and get the earnest money back. The seller keeps the option fee.</p>
<p>Accepting the property as-is does not stop the buyer from inspecting, from negotiating an amendment based on what the inspection finds, or from terminating during the option period. A typical as-is negotiation looks like this:</p>
<ol>
<li>You accept an as-is offer with a short option period.</li>
<li>The buyer's inspector finds a problem, such as foundation movement or an old electrical panel.</li>
<li>The buyer asks for a repair, a credit or a lower price.</li>
<li>You agree, counter or decline. If you decline, the buyer may terminate before the option period ends.</li>
</ol>
<p>You can limit this by negotiating a shorter option period, a higher option fee, or no option period at all, but each of those makes your offer terms less attractive to buyers. Problems you disclosed up front carry less weight in renegotiation, because the buyer already priced them in.</p>

<h2>Buyers still inspect, so get ahead of it</h2>
<p>Expect every serious as-is buyer to order an inspection, and many investors will bring contractors too. Knowing what they will find helps you price correctly and avoid a renegotiation shock.</p>
<p>Consider a pre-listing inspection, or at least a walk-through with simple tools. A GFCI outlet tester and a moisture meter catch the kinds of issues inspectors flag every day, such as ungrounded outlets and damp drywall from a slow leak.</p>
[[product:outlet-tester]]
<p>In the Houston area, inspectors and buyers pay especially close attention to a few systems:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Foundation and drainage</strong>, since expansive clay soils and poor grading can lead to slab movement.</li>
<li><strong>Roof condition and age</strong>, which also affects the buyer's ability to get homeowners insurance.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC</strong>, because a failing system in a Texas summer is an immediate cost.</li>
<li><strong>Signs of past water intrusion</strong>, from roof leaks, plumbing or flooding.</li>
</ul>
<p>Also remember that "as-is" does not change the buyer's lender. If your buyer is using an FHA, VA or conventional loan, the appraiser and lender may still require certain conditions to be fixed before they will fund. That is one reason as-is listings with significant defects tend to attract cash and investor buyers.</p>
<p>You do not have to fix what you find. The goal is to disclose it accurately and price it in, so the buyer has fewer surprises to negotiate over.</p>

<h2>Disclosure is still required when selling as-is</h2>
<p>An as-is clause deals with repairs and warranties. It does not replace the seller's disclosure notice. Texas Property Code Section 5.008 requires sellers of most single-family resales to give the buyer a written notice on or before the contract's effective date, covering known defects, past repairs, flooding, insurance claims for flood damage and more.</p>
<p>Some transfers are exempt, including foreclosure sales and sales by an executor administering an estate. Even then, actively concealing a known defect can lead to fraud claims, and as the Prudential case shows, fraud can defeat an as-is clause. Our complete guide to the <a href="/blog/texas-seller-disclosure-notice/">Texas seller disclosure notice</a> covers the form section by section.</p>

<h2>Cash offer vs. listing as-is: running the math</h2>
<p>When you sell as-is, you usually have two paths: list it with an agent and market it as-is, or sell directly to a cash buyer or investor. Compare them by net proceeds and time, not by the offer price alone. The example below uses made-up round numbers purely to show the method. Your figures will differ.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Line item (hypothetical)</th><th>List as-is with agent</th><th>Direct cash offer</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sale price</td><td>$300,000</td><td>$255,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agent commission (assumed for this example)</td><td>-$15,000</td><td>$0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seller closing costs and title (assumed)</td><td>-$4,500</td><td>-$1,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repair credit after inspection (assumed)</td><td>-$5,000</td><td>$0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extra months of mortgage, taxes, insurance, utilities (assumed)</td><td>-$6,000</td><td>-$1,500</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Estimated net</strong></td><td><strong>$269,500</strong></td><td><strong>$252,000</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In this example, listing nets more, but the cash sale closes faster with less uncertainty. If the house needed major repairs that scared off financed buyers, or carrying costs were higher, the gap could shrink or flip. Commissions are negotiable, and who pays which closing costs is set by the contract. See our breakdown of <a href="/blog/closing-costs-in-texas/">closing costs in Texas</a>, then plug your real numbers into the <a href="/tools/seller-net-proceeds-calculator/">seller net proceeds calculator</a>.</p>
<p>When comparing cash offers, watch for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Offers that drop sharply after the buyer's inspection.</li>
<li>Long option periods that tie up your house while the buyer looks for a resale buyer.</li>
<li>Small earnest money deposits, which signal low commitment.</li>
<li>Closing through anyone other than a Texas title company.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When selling as-is makes sense</h2>
<p>Selling as-is is a strategy, not a sign of failure. It tends to make sense when:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Repairs cost more than they add in value</strong>, or you simply do not have the cash to make them.</li>
<li><strong>The house has major damage</strong> from fire, flood or a foundation problem. See our guide to <a href="/blog/sell-fire-damaged-house-texas/">selling a fire damaged house in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>You inherited the property</strong> and live out of state, or heirs want a clean, quick split.</li>
<li><strong>Time matters more than top dollar</strong>, such as a job relocation, divorce or two mortgage payments.</li>
<li><strong>You are a landlord</strong> selling a tired rental and do not want to manage a renovation.</li>
</ul>
<p>It makes less sense when the house needs only cosmetic work. Paint, landscaping, a deep clean and small fixes often return more than they cost, and they widen your buyer pool to financed buyers.</p>
<p>Before signing an as-is contract or any amendment, consider having a Texas real estate attorney review the terms that matter to you.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Selling a house as-is in Texas means no promised repairs and no warranties beyond the contract, but buyers can still inspect, negotiate and walk away during the option period, and you still owe the seller's disclosure notice. Disclose honestly, price for the condition, and compare listing against cash offers by net proceeds. Do that, and as-is can be the simplest path to a clean sale.</p>
